Yes, that's the standard approach without a data-fetching library. One thing to make sure you handle: race conditions.

If the user clicks experiment A, then quickly clicks B, the A request might return after B's and overwrite the panel with the wrong data. The cleanup function prevents that:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (selectedId == null) return;
  const controller = new AbortController();

  fetch(`/api/experiments/${selectedId}/measurements`, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(setMeasurements)
    .catch(err => {
      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setError(err);
    });

  return () => controller.abort();
}, [selectedId]);
```

That also fixes the StrictMode double-fetch issue from before, since the first request gets aborted.
